iances among the groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups or treatments. It is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher has more than two groups to compare.\n- The experimental design allows for the assessment of multiple factors simultaneously (in factorial designs).\n- The assumptions of ANOVA are satisfied.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for ANOVA typically involves:\n1. Calculating the total sum of squares (SST), the sum of squares for treatments (SSTr), and the sum of squares for error (SSE).\n2. Computing the mean squares for treatments (MST) and for error (MSE):\n   \\[\n   MST = \\frac{SSTr}{k-1}, \\quad MSE = \\frac{SSE}{N-k}\n   \\]\n   where \\( k \\) is the number of groups and \\( N \\) is the total number of observations.\n3. Performing an F-test to compare the mean squares:\n   \\[\n   F = \\frac{MST}{MSE}\n   \\]\n4. Interpreting the F-statistic against a critical value from the F-distribution to determine significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA allows for the comparison of multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error associated with multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a clear framework for understanding the sources of variation in the data.\n- ANOVA can be extended to more complex designs, such as factorial ANOVA and repeated measures ANOVA.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n- The interpretation of results can be complex in the presence of interactions among factors.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include t-tests for comparing two groups, MANOVA for multivariate responses, and regression analysis for model